Poll for interrupts more often in fuzzing builds.

eval() checks for interrupts, time limits and pending finalizers once per
1000 evaluations, and the byte-code interpreter once per 1024
instructions. That check is the only point at which a fuzz harness can
stop a runaway input: the eval target bounds each input's work from
R_PolledEvents, which is reached from exactly there. One evaluation can
be a whole-heap vector operation, so a thousand of them can run for
minutes between two checks, long past the fuzzer's timeout threshold,
while the budget sees nothing.

Under FUZZING_BUILD_MODE_UNSAFE_FOR_PRODUCTION (defined by every OSS-Fuzz
build) poll every 10 evaluations and every 128 byte-code instructions
instead. Interrupt checks are cheap next to sanitizer instrumentation,
and production builds are unchanged.

This is a fuzzing-mode adjustment, not a bug fix: it is not meant for
upstream and stays carried for as long as the eval target depends on it
(see docs/eval.md). The harness degrades gracefully without it -- its
CPU backstop still bounds each input -- but vector-heavy inputs then run
for seconds rather than milliseconds.

